{"name":"Favourite Afternoon","hex":"#ffe9ba","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"favourite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Most-liked. (In other words, [person]'s favorite [noun] = the [noun] that [person] likes the most.)","Liked, cherished."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person or thing who enjoys special regard or favour.","A person who is preferred or trusted above all others."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/favourite"},{"term":"afternoon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The part of the day from noon or lunchtime until sunset, evening, or suppertime or 6pm.","The later part of anything, often with implications of decline."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["In the afternoon."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/afternoon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Favourite","blocks":[{"text":"In history and politics, a favourite was the intimate companion of a ruler or other important person. In post-classical and early-modern Europe, among other times and places, the term was used of individuals delegated significant political power by a ruler. It was especially a phenomenon of the 16th and 17th centuries, when government had become too complex for many hereditary rulers with no great interest in or talent for it, and political institutions were still evolving. From 1600 to 1660 there were particular successions of all-powerful minister-favourites in much of Europe, particularly in Spain, England, France and Sweden."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Count-Duke_of_Olivares.jpg/330px-Count-Duke_of_Olivares.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Favourite","wikidata":"Q1996375"},{"title":"Afternoon","blocks":[{"text":"Afternoon is the period from noon to sunset or evening. It is the time when the sun is descending from its peak in the sky to somewhat before its terminus at the horizon in the west. In human life, it occupies roughly the latter half of the standard work and school day. In literal terms, it refers to a time specifically after noon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Wien_T%C3%BCrkenschanzpark_%282376611600%29.jpg/330px-Wien_T%C3%BCrkenschanzpark_%282376611600%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afternoon","wikidata":"Q283102"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Count-Duke_of_Olivares.jpg/500px-Count-Duke_of_Olivares.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Count-Duke_of_Olivares.jpg/1280px-Count-Duke_of_Olivares.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Count-Duke_of_Olivares.jpg/500px-Count-Duke_of_Olivares.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Equestrian portrait of the Count-Duke of Olivares by Diego Velázquez (c. 1636)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACount-Duke_of_Olivares.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Rubens%29.jpg/500px-Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Rubens%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Rubens%29.jpg/1280px-Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Rubens%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Portrait_of_George_Villiers%2C_1st_Duke_of_Buckingham_%28by_Peter_Paul_Ruben